1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:00,980
qwenTPU.

2
00:00:00,980 --> 00:00:07,501
A weight stationary matrix multiply array, compiled into the weights of a stock Qwen three

3
00:00:07,501 --> 00:00:08,298
point five.

4
00:00:08,298 --> 00:00:14,675
No TPU hardware and no training: a language model that does exact matrix multiplication.

5
00:00:15,670 --> 00:00:17,795
A TPU is mostly memory plus adders.

6
00:00:17,795 --> 00:00:23,199
A Gated DeltaNet head, the linear attention layer in Qwen three point five, keeps a state

7
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:27,813
matrix, and one multi hot read returns the exact sum of the rows you select.

8
00:00:27,813 --> 00:00:31,031
So the head already is the memory and the adder tree.

9
00:00:32,030 --> 00:00:34,853
Weights are written into the state as tokens, once.

10
00:00:34,853 --> 00:00:39,946
Then each activation selects rows, the adder tree sums them in one read, and a top down fold

11
00:00:39,946 --> 00:00:43,378
with saturating clamps keeps the integers exact in bf sixteen.

12
00:00:43,378 --> 00:00:46,201
Chained layers read the model's own sampled tokens.

13
00:00:46,201 --> 00:00:51,405
Qwen's original layers are muted for these tokens, so the chip leaves no trace in the language

14
00:00:51,405 --> 00:00:52,291
model's context.

15
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:54,632
Four things are new.

16
00:00:54,632 --> 00:00:58,656
A dataflow array rather than a CPU, with no program counter.

17
00:00:58,656 --> 00:01:01,339
One multi hot read as a whole reduction.

18
00:01:01,339 --> 00:01:04,358
Exact integers up to five bits in bf sixteen.

19
00:01:04,358 --> 00:01:08,919
And zero trace, because Qwen's own layers are muted for chip tokens.

20
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:17,729
The headline: 1,361,280 replayed token positions across 5 different machines, and 0 mismatches

21
00:01:17,729 --> 00:01:18,892
against NumPy.

22
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:21,771
Replay volume per device.

23
00:01:21,771 --> 00:01:27,188
The same suite ran on an A100, an A10G, an L4, a T4 and an x86 Xeon CPU.

24
00:01:27,188 --> 00:01:33,960
Running on different hardware matters: it caught a bf sixteen rounding issue and a split K

25
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:38,474
problem that a float sixty four simulation could never show.

26
00:01:39,470 --> 00:01:47,875
On an A100 with the zero point eight B model, running the whole network gives 6,629 tokens per

27
00:01:47,875 --> 00:01:48,501
second.

28
00:01:48,501 --> 00:01:56,728
Skipping Qwen's muted layers gives 74,462, which is 11, and one CUDA graph per trace reaches

29
00:01:56,728 --> 00:01:57,980
about 340,925.

30
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:02,451
Free running chained matvecs reach 705 per second.

31
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:09,423
Version two adds a tiny instruction set, an exact relu and a sequencer, so the model emits

32
00:02:09,423 --> 00:02:13,007
every token itself and the prompt chooses the network.

33
00:02:13,007 --> 00:02:16,657
In the v two bench, all 196 of 196 programs were exact.

34
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:18,429
Honest limits.

35
00:02:18,429 --> 00:02:23,484
It is not faster than QwenPU per operation, since it does four to sixty four MACs per token.

36
00:02:23,484 --> 00:02:24,968
Version one is host driven.

37
00:02:24,968 --> 00:02:30,078
On the sixteen gigabyte T4 only 9 of 12 suite runs fit in memory, though the replay is exact.

38
00:02:30,078 --> 00:02:33,815
And teacher forced numbers are prefill throughput, not free running.

39
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:38,599
The code, the write-up, and every log are in the repo.

